 ## Overview
-Discrete Multi-Material Interface Reconstruction (MIR) is a method to reconstruct material interfaces from volume fraction data by discretizing cells into subcells, labeling them with material IDs, and optimizing the labeling to minimize interface energy while preserving volume fractions [S1, unofficial]. It improves upon the [Volume-Adaptive Active Interface Model](13766-volume-adaptive-active-interface-model.md) by producing interfaces that are continuous across cell boundaries and suitable for visualization, whereas the parent model and alternatives like Piecewise Linear Interface Calculation (PLIC) are discontinuous across cell boundaries [S1, unofficial]. Unlike PLIC, which is ambiguous for three or more materials due to the ordering of its binary segmentations, MIR explicitly handles $n$-material problems through a discrete labeling optimization [S1, unofficial].
+Discrete Multi-Material Interface Reconstruction (MIR) reconstructs material interfaces from volume fraction data by discretizing cells into subcells, labeling them with material IDs, and optimizing the labeling to minimize interface energy while preserving volume fractions [S1, unofficial]. It produces interfaces continuous across cell boundaries suitable for visualization, unlike the discontinuous [Volume-Adaptive Active Interface Model](13766-volume-adaptive-active-interface-model.md) and Piecewise Linear Interface Calculation (PLIC) [S1, unofficial].
 
